# SCRIPT: ST3D-BUILD-V1 — The_Step_3D_v1_20261118.html: three.js and the parts kit (mixer3d_parts_v1, from his ball pages, unchanged),
# the frames from Steve McCormick's fourteen step runs (ST3D-EXPORT-V1), ST3D-LAYER-V1.
kit = ''.join(open('../hsb/ball_blocks.html', encoding='utf-8').readlines()[:231])
frames = open('st3d_frames_v1.json', encoding='utf-8').read()
head = '''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>The Step</title>
<style>
:root{color-scheme:dark;--bg:#050c18;--panel:#0b1524;--line:#1f2f44;--dim:#8fa3ba;--ink:#e7f0fb;--teal:#4fe0c0;--mono:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace}*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);font:13px/1.45 system-ui,Segoe UI,Roboto,sans-serif;height:100vh;display:grid;grid-template-columns:310px 1fr;overflow:hidden}
aside{background:var(--panel);border-right:1px solid var(--line);padding:12px;overflow:auto}aside h1{font-size:14px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0 0 2px}aside .by{color:var(--dim);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px}
.g{margin:9px 0 3px;color:var(--dim);font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}.g+div{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:4px;align-items:center}
button{font:inherit;font-size:12px;padding:3px 8px;background:#0e1a2b;color:inherit;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;cursor:pointer}button[aria-pressed=true]{border-color:var(--teal);color:var(--teal)}
input[type=number]{width:60px;font:inherit;font-size:12px;background:#0e1a2b;color:inherit;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:5px;padding:3px 5px}input[type=range]{width:100%}
#num .row{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;padding:4px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(31,47,68,.7);color:var(--dim);font-size:11.5px}#num .row b{font-family:var(--mono);font-weight:400;color:var(--ink);font-size:10.5px}
canvas.ch{width:100%;display:block;background:rgba(9,13,20,.6);border-radius:5px;margin-top:3px}#c0{height:150px}#c1{height:120px}
details{margin-top:10px}summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--dim);font-size:11px}details p{color:var(--dim);font-size:11.5px;line-height:1.55;margin:6px 0}
#stage{position:relative;overflow:hidden}#scene{width:100%;height:100%;display:block}#labels{position:absolute;inset:0;pointer-events:none}
.lab,.head,.rowl{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;transform:translate(-100vw,-100vh);text-align:center;text-shadow:0 0 6px #000;white-space:nowrap}
.lab{color:var(--dim);font-size:11px;margin-left:-60px;width:120px}.lab b{font-weight:600}.lab .bh{color:#fff}.lab .ds{color:#63d3ff}
.head{color:#dfe7ef;font-size:12px;margin-left:-70px;width:140px;margin-top:-30px}.head span{color:var(--dim);font-size:10.5px}
.rowl{color:#dfe7ef;font-size:11.5px;margin-left:-60px;width:120px;margin-top:-26px;line-height:1.25}.rowl span{color:var(--dim);font-size:10px}
@media (max-width:820px){body{grid-template-columns:1fr;grid-template-rows:auto 1fr}aside{max-height:46vh;border-right:0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}}
</style></head><body>
<aside><h1>The Step</h1><div class="by">Jeromie Beasley · 18 Nov 2026 · AI-assisted<br>Physics, code and runs: Stephen McCormick</div>
<div class="g">Time ε (horizon crossing at 1)</div><div><input id="eps" type="range" min="0.1" max="6.5" step="0.005" value="0.1"><button id="play">Pause</button><button id="restart">From the start</button><label style="color:var(--dim);font-size:11px;flex-basis:100%">speed <input id="speed" type="range" min="0.1" max="3" step="0.1" value="1"></label></div>
<div class="g">Jump to</div><div><button data-jump="0.5">ε 0.5</button><button data-jump="2">ε 2</button><button data-jump="3.4">ε 3.4</button><button data-jump="3.92">hot horizons 3.9</button><button data-jump="4.44">2 MeV horizon 4.43</button><button data-jump="6.2">end</button></div>
<div class="g">Rows</div><div><button data-show="pf">Neutrinos held in</button><button data-show="nu">Neutrinos leave</button></div>
<div class="g">Show</div><div><button data-show="shells">Shells</button><button data-show="clocks">Clocks</button><button data-show="fans">Neutrino fans</button><button data-show="names">Names</button></div>
<div class="g">View</div><div>zoom <input id="zoom" type="number" value="100"> % <button id="zout">−</button><button id="zin">+</button> pan <input id="panx" type="number" value="0"> % <input id="pany" type="number" value="0"> % <button id="reset">Reset</button></div>
<div class="g">His threshold δc against horizon mass (grey held in, blue his physical curve; circles: the 14 lumps, white once a horizon forms)</div><canvas class="ch" id="c0"></canvas>
<div class="g">Peak compaction C_max against ε (dashed grey held in, blue neutrinos leave; brighter = hotter; white dot: horizon)</div><canvas class="ch" id="c1"></canvas>
<div class="g">Numbers, by column</div><div id="num"></div>
<details><summary>What is drawn</summary><p>Steve McCormick’s step, in three dimensions. One lump, the same initial data at amplitude δm = 0.515, is evolved by his code at seven horizon masses, from T_H = 16 MeV (585 solar masses) on the left to T_H = 0.0625 MeV (6.7×10⁷ solar masses) on the right. The top row keeps the neutrinos in the fluid throughout: his perfect fluid with the e± equation of state. The bottom row is his full model: plasma and neutrinos are one fluid while his collision rate is more than a hundred times the expansion rate, then they split, and the neutrinos are carried by his transport equation with collisions at κ = H(T_b/T_dec)³(T/T_b)⁵, T_dec = 1 MeV.</p>
<p>Held in, the lump forms a black hole at every mass. With the neutrinos leaving, it forms one at 16, 4 and 2 MeV, where they are still held when the lump collapses, and bounces at 1 MeV and below, where they stream out of it and carry pressure support away: the threshold steps up from 0.498 to about 0.52, and δm = 0.515 sits on the step. The first chart is his threshold curve, the one in his Figure 4 and Table VII, with this lump’s amplitude as the gold line.</p>
<p>Each lump is 14 of his 300 plasma shells (r from 0.12 to 3.2 in units of the lump’s size r_m), drawn at R/a, the background expansion divided out. Colour is his compaction C = 2(M − M_b)/R: deep blue at C ≤ 0, teal 0.25, gold 0.5, orange 0.75, red at 1; a shell turns white when it falls in with 2M/R ≥ 1, the apparent horizon, where his code stops. The bead on each shell is its clock, turning at his lapse A (A = 1 at the outer cell). On the bottom row the fans on every second shell (r = 0.42 to 2.1) are the neutrino intensity I(μ) over its mean, against the outward direction: round when isotropic, stretched out when streaming. A fan is bright orange where that shell’s own κ/H is below 1 and pale where it is above 1. The compressed core can hold its neutrinos again (κ/H ∝ T⁵) while the background has let them go.</p></details>
<details><summary>His equations, as the page uses them</summary><p>Metric ds² = −A²dt² + B²dr² + R²dΩ², r labelling the plasma shells, Γ² = 1 + U² − 2M/R. Plasma: D_te = −(e + p)θ with his tabulated e± equation of state, (e + p)g = −D_rp + κq. Neutrinos: his transport equation for I(t, r, μ), moments e_ν, q, P_r, collisions relaxing I toward isotropy at κ = H_b(T_b/T_dec)³(T/T_b)⁵ and handing their momentum to the plasma. The lapse is integrated inward from the acceleration, A = 1 at the outer cell. Compaction C = 2(M − M_b)/R. Apparent horizon: 2M/R ≥ 1 with U &lt; 0. Horizon mass from his mass-function code, M_H = c³/(2GH) at T_H. Steve’s Γ is the radial gradient of the areal radius; it shares a letter with the peel step Γ = tanh(r/2) and nothing else.</p></details>
<details><summary>Checked before this page ran (ST3D-CHECK-01)</summary><p>The fourteen runs are his code (PBH_neutrino_simulation, commit eb7c4de, 11 Nov 2026) called exactly as his bisections call it, with nothing in his loop changed; the frames are taken by wrapping his right-hand side from outside. (a) Six runs have a twin line in his archived bisection logs, and each ends the same way at the same ε to his two decimals: 16 MeV BH 3.79, 4 MeV BH 3.91, 2 MeV BH 4.43, 1 MeV dispersed 4.89, 0.5 MeV dispersed 4.58, and the held-in 1 MeV run at δm = 0.500, BH 4.87. (b) In all seven full-model runs his own snapshot at ε = 2 equals our recorded frame with no difference at all. (c) All fifteen outcomes lie on the side of his archived threshold that they must. (d) His physical threshold curve matches Table VII of the paper on every row.</p></details>
<details><summary>Next to the moving matrix of balls (structural parallels, not identities)</summary><p>Each lump is layers inside one ball, each layer with its own clock; the clocks slow toward the squeezed centre. Whether the neutrinos move with the plasma or leave it is set by a ratio of two rates, κ against H, and the step is where that ratio passes through one while the lump is collapsing. The horizon is where a shell reaches 2M/R = 1. These are parallels of structure; the page draws his physics and nothing of the ball model.</p></details>
<details><summary>Limits</summary><p>Spherical symmetry, as in his paper. The bottom row is his single-rate (gray) model with T_dec = 1 MeV; his physical curve in the chart uses his six-group model between 0.3 and 4 MeV, which differs from the single-rate thresholds by less than 0.0015 and puts δm = 0.515 on the same side at every column drawn. Only 14 of his shells are drawn, fans only on every second shell from r = 0.42 to 2.1, and the lumps are scaled by 0.4 and placed in a grid for comparison only. His code stops at the first apparent horizon, and at dispersal (after ε = 3, once C_max falls below half its peak); nothing after is followed here.</p></details>
</aside>
<main id="stage"><canvas id="scene"></canvas><div id="labels"></div></main>
'''
s = head + kit + '\n<script>window.ST3D_FRAMES = ' + frames + ';</script>\n<script>' + open('st3d_layer_v1.js', encoding='utf-8').read() + '</script>\n</body></html>'
open('The_Step_3D_v1_20261118.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s); print(len(s))
